00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:22.970
<v Jeff Simmermon>So it's some time in college, and I'm going through that first big break-up that everybody goes through, that sort of marks their transition into adulthood. And I'm just sitting there on my bed. And I've got my face in my hands and I'm listening to The Smiths over and over again and just torturing myself and just going, "ooooh."
<v Jeff Simmermon>那是在大学期间的某个时候，我正在经历每个人都会经历的第一次大分手，这标志着他们向成年人的转变。我正坐在床上。我双手掩面，一遍又一遍地听着史密斯乐队的歌，折磨自己，一直在说，“噢”。

00:41:22.970 --> 00:41:56.640
<v Jeff Simmermon>And my little sister comes into my room and sits down on the bed next to me. And she just gives me this huge hug. She puts her arms around me, and she takes a Kleenex out, and dabs my tears, and said, "Jeff, listen. Bitches gonna come and bitches gonna go. But you've been there for me since the day I was born, and that kind of love, that's forever. I'm gonna love you till after you are dead, and I am not lying."
<v Jeff Simmermon>我的小妹妹走进我的房间，坐在我旁边的床上。她给了我一个大大的拥抱。她用手臂搂住我，拿出一张纸巾，擦干我的眼泪，说：“杰夫，听着。婊子会来，婊子会走。但从我出生的那天起你就一直在我身边，那种爱，是永恒的。我会爱你直到你死后，我没撒谎。”

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:57.620
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声]

00:41:57.620 --> 00:41:58.590
<v Jeff Simmermon>For real.
<v Jeff Simmermon>真的。

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:01.030
<v Jeff Simmermon>[APPLAUSE AND LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[掌声和笑声]

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:30.610
<v Jeff Simmermon>And then, she continues and says, "And I just wanted you to know that I have always hated that bitch. And if you want, I'll be happy, more than happy, to go around to her crib and whip her ass with a dog chain one time. You just say the word." I just took the hug.
<v Jeff Simmermon>然后，她继续说，“我只是想让你知道，我一直很讨厌那个婊子。如果你想，我很乐意，非常乐意去她的住处，用狗链狠狠抽她一顿。你只要说一声。”我就接受了这个拥抱。

00:42:30.610 --> 00:43:09.130
<v Jeff Simmermon>What you need to understand is that we have the same parents. We grew up in the same home. She was in the 9th grade. Because while I was off at college studying art and listening to Sonic Youth, she and her friends were on another track. And she was invited to join a gang, but we've never been into group things, she just freelanced. And she and her friends used to break into rich people's houses. And they would drink 40s, and take pictures of it, and leave the 40s and whatever, and just leave.
<v Jeff Simmermon>你需要了解的是，我们有相同的父母。我们在同一个家里长大。她读九年级。因为当我在大学学习艺术并听着音速青年乐队时，她和她的朋友们走在另一条路上。她曾被邀请加入一个帮派，但我们从不参与团体活动，她只是单干。她和她的朋友们过去常闯入有钱人的房子。他们会喝40盎司装的啤酒，拍照，留下空瓶子什么的，然后直接走人。

00:43:09.130 --> 00:43:11.580
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声]

00:43:11.580 --> 00:43:42.060
<v Jeff Simmermon>And her boyfriend at the time, his winter coat and his mustache had this inversely proportional size relationship where his coat, sort of, advertised a faraway hockey team and he had this nasty little weasel penis draped across his top lip. And just something in his eyes was that dangerous combination of weak and predatory.
<v Jeff Simmermon>当时的男朋友，他的冬装外套和他的胡子有种反比例关系，他的外套广告着一个遥远的冰球队，而他的上唇上留着那种令人讨厌的黄鼠狼胡子。他眼睛里的某种东西是软弱和掠夺性的危险组合。

00:43:42.060 --> 00:44:22.640
<v Jeff Simmermon>And so I was working as an art teacher at her high school as a practicum thing in order to graduate. And I'm walking down the hall one day, and I hear the laughter that I was very accustomed to hearing from back when I was in high school. And I see her boyfriend and some of his similarly parka'd friends shoving somebody into a locker. And they're just shoving him, and laughing, and shoving him, and laughing. And I get up to them, and I'm just kind of sheep-dogging kids off to class. And I'm like, what is this bull [BLEEP]. Go on! Go to class! Go! Go! Go! And I look, and the kid that they're shoving up against the locker is my little sister.
<v Jeff Simmermon>所以我当时在她的高中做艺术老师，作为毕业前的实习。有一天，我走在走廊里，听到了我上高中时非常熟悉的笑声。我看到她的男朋友和一些同样穿着帕克大衣的朋友把某人塞进储物柜。他们一直在推他，大笑，再推他，再大笑。我走过去，像牧羊犬一样把孩子们赶去上课。我说，这是什么胡扯。走开！去上课！快！快！快！我一看，被他们推向储物柜的孩子是我的小妹妹。

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:51.740
<v Jeff Simmermon>And she has that look on her face, that I know so well, where her jaw is really set and her eyes are huge and wet. And she is definitely not crying. She is fine. And having been responsible for that look in the past, I know what it takes to provoke that. And I was like, are you OK? Are you OK? Do you need me to do something? And she's like, "I'm fine! [BLEEP] I gotta go! [BLEEP]!" and then just leaves.
<v Jeff Simmermon>她脸上有着我非常熟悉的表情，下巴紧绷，眼睛又大又湿。她绝对没有哭。她没事。曾经导致那种表情的我，知道需要什么才能激怒她。我说，你没事吧？你没事吧？需要我做什么吗？她说，“我没事！[哔——]我得走了！[哔——]！”然后就离开了。

00:44:51.740 --> 00:45:09.740
<v Jeff Simmermon>And I just, instantly, burn with a white-hot rage. And I have all sorts of elaborate and horrific revenge fantasies that involve roping the dude to the back of my bumper and dragging him up and down the highway. Or covering him with gas and setting him on fire, whatever.
<v Jeff Simmermon>然后我立刻燃起了炽热的愤怒。我有各种各样精致而可怕的复仇幻想，包括把那家伙绑在我的保险杠后面，沿着高速公路把他拖来拖去。或者给他浇上汽油并点火，随便什么。

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:37.720
<v Jeff Simmermon>And it's very difficult to teach that day. And I don't know what to do, but this aggression will not stand, man. I gotta do something. And then I'm coming back from my planning bell, and I'm coming up the stairs, and I see him standing there on the stairwell. And the window is open, and he's looking out the window. And he's got this dopey little smile on his open mouth. And the breeze is playfully ruffling his mullet off of his shoulders.
<v Jeff Simmermon>那天很难教书。我不知道该怎么办，但这股侵略性不会就这样结束，伙计。我得做点什么。然后我在计划课后回来，走上楼梯，看到他站在楼梯间。窗户开着，他正看着窗外。他张开的嘴上带着傻乎乎的小笑容。微风顽皮地吹乱了他肩膀上的鲻鱼头。

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:44.630
<v Jeff Simmermon>And I get up to him, and I just drop my hand on his shoulder, and I spin him around and I say, "Hey, man! I saw you in the hall with my sister." And then I run out of plan.
<v Jeff Simmermon>我走到他面前，把手放在他的肩膀上，让他转过身来说，“嘿，伙计！我在走廊里看到你和我妹妹在一起了。”然后我就没词了。

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:45.170
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声]

00:45:45.170 --> 00:46:16.010
<v Jeff Simmermon>Because I've been spending all day daydreaming about all this awesome, cinematic stuff I want to do and not really figuring out what would happen if I actually got to confront him, which is weird since we're in the same building all day. And I can't think of anything. I'm just pulling him in closer. And I look him in the eye, I was like, "I saw you in the hall with my sister." And I want to bite his lips off and just spit them on the floor. And I can't exactly do that, so I just give him a big, long kiss right on the mouth. And I keep my eyes open, and I lock eyes with him. And I can feel his mustache against my back lip. And the whole time I'm thinking, dominate and intimidate.
<v Jeff Simmermon>因为我整天都在幻想我想做的那些酷炫的电影般的事情，并没有真正考虑到如果我真的面对他会发生什么，这很奇怪，因为我们整天在同一栋楼里。我想不出什么。我只是把他拉近一点。我直视他的眼睛，我说，“我在走廊里看到你和我妹妹在一起了。”我真想咬掉他的嘴唇，直接吐在地上。我做不到，所以我只是在他的嘴上重重地吻了一口。我睁着眼睛，与他对视。我能感觉到他的胡子擦着我的后唇。整个过程中我一直在想，支配和恐吓。

00:46:16.010 --> 00:46:18.250
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声]

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:27.970
<v Jeff Simmermon>And I pushed him back. And I was like, "Um, look, it's cool." I can't think of anything to say. And I was just like, "You can come over whenever you want. I look forward to seeing you around. Goodbye."
<v Jeff Simmermon>然后我推开他。我说，“嗯，看，这很酷。”我想不出什么可说的。我就像，“你可以随时过来。我期待着见到你。再见。”

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:28.960
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声]

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:58.870
<v Jeff Simmermon>And split. And I just kind of go home. And later that night, my sister came home. And they had met up after school and had a big fight. And they'd broken up. But she looked at me, she said, "Mother [BLEEP], are you out of your rabid-ass mind? What were you even thinking?" And I said, "Jess, bitches gonna come and bitches gonna go, but you have been there for me since the day you were born. And that kind of love, that is real, and it is lifelong. And I'm gonna love you till after you are dead. And I am not even lying, for real."
<v Jeff Simmermon>然后闪人。我只是回了家。那天晚上晚些时候，我妹妹回家了。他们放学后见了面，打了一大架。他们分手了。但她看着我说，“妈的，你的脑子是让狗吃了吗？你到底在想什么？”我说，“杰西，婊子会来，婊子会走，但从你出生的那天起你就一直在我身边。那种爱，是真实的，是终身的。我会爱你直到你死后。我真的没撒谎。”

00:46:58.870 --> 00:47:03.710
<v Jeff Simmermon>[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]
<v Jeff Simmermon>[笑声和掌声]

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:16.240
<v Ira Glass>So Jeff, for a minute, joins me in the studio. Jeff, your story raises some important questions, and I want to go through those with you right now. First of all, why the kiss?
<v Ira Glass>杰夫，有一分钟加入我的演播室。杰夫，你的故事引发了一些重要的问题，我想现在和你谈谈。首先，为什么亲他？

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:46.680
<v Jeff Simmermon>It was all that I could think of at the time. I was very limited in what I could do, you know? Had I pursued violence-- this is the kind of guy, he rolled around with a crew. Drive-bys happened in my hometown a lot. And had I beaten him up like I wanted to-- I don't really know how to beat people up-- he could have come back later with some sort of drive-by scenario. Or he could have taken his bruises, or whatever, to the administration, I could never teach again. So I had to do something that was completely off the map.
<v Jeff Simmermon>那是当时我唯一能想到的。我的选择非常有限，你知道吗？如果我追求暴力——他是那种带着帮派的人。在我家乡经常发生免下车枪击案。如果我像我想的那样打他一顿——我真的不知道怎么打人——他可能后来带着某种枪击场景回来。或者他可能带着伤，什么的，向行政部门举报，我就永远不能再教书了。所以我必须做一些完全出格的事情。

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:49.690
<v Ira Glass>Could we talk about the moment after you kiss him?
<v Ira Glass>我们可以谈谈你吻他之后的那一刻吗？

00:47:49.690 --> 00:47:50.460
<v Jeff Simmermon>Sure.
<v Jeff Simmermon>当然。

00:47:50.460 --> 00:47:52.830
<v Ira Glass>Were you just as surprised as he was?
<v Ira Glass>你当时和他一样惊讶吗？

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:54.340
<v Jeff Simmermon>Yes. Possibly more so.
<v Jeff Simmermon>是的。可能更甚。

00:47:54.340 --> 00:47:56.070
<v Ira Glass>Did you know you had this in you?
<v Ira Glass>你知道你体内有这一面吗？

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:57.110
<v Jeff Simmermon>No.
<v Jeff Simmermon>不知道。

00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:02.250
<v Ira Glass>And how did you feel after you did it? Did you feel like, yes, score?
<v Ira Glass>你做完之后感觉如何？你觉得，耶，得分了？

00:48:02.250 --> 00:48:14.370
<v Jeff Simmermon>No. I felt terrified. I felt that I am past the point of no return right now. And whatever happens, there's no counsel for me.
<v Jeff Simmermon>没有。我觉得很害怕。我觉得我现在已经过了无法回头的点了。无论发生什么，我都没有后路。

00:48:14.370 --> 00:48:25.770
<v Ira Glass>If you had to summarize in a sentence your message to him with that kiss, like why you chose the kiss, what would you say?
<v Ira Glass>如果你要用一句话总结你那个吻传递给他的信息，就像你为什么选择吻他，你会怎么说？

00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:34.940
<v Jeff Simmermon>You've opened the lid on forces that nobody knows how to deal with. Close the box and step away.
<v Jeff Simmermon>你已经打开了没人知道怎么处理的力量的盖子。关上盒子，退后。

00:48:34.940 --> 00:48:50.180
<v Ira Glass>So with this kid, you decide you're going to take the law into your own hands. You're going to be the one to fix this. You're going to set this right. What if you had tried to protect her in a more traditional way? You know, go to one of the other teachers, or the principal, and just get him suspended or expelled or something?
<v Ira Glass>所以在这个孩子身上，你决定你要把法律掌握在自己手中。你要成为解决这件事的人。你要纠正这件事。如果你尝试用更传统的方式保护她呢？你知道，去找其他老师或者校长，让他被停课或开除什么的？

00:48:50.180 --> 00:49:13.140
<v Jeff Simmermon>Because it wouldn't have worked. He wouldn't have cared. He didn't come to school enough for a suspension to be a meaningful change in his life one way or the other. I think he wouldn't have gotten the message. I don't think he would've gotten it. I think life would have continued unabated. He may have continued dating my sister, or may have continued shoving her around. Nothing would have stopped. Nothing would have changed. There had to be change.
<v Jeff Simmermon>因为那不会起作用。他不会在乎。他没去学校上课，停课对他来说不会是生活中的任何重大改变。我想他不会收到信息的。我认为他不会明白。我想生活会继续下去，不受影响。他可能继续和我妹妹约会，或者继续推推搡搡她。什么都不会停止。什么都不会改变。必须有所改变。

00:49:13.140 --> 00:49:54.740
<v Ira Glass>Jeff Simmermon, he does other stories on his blog, andiamnotlying.com. That's and I am not lying, all one word. Thanks, as always, to The Moth, which uses personal stories told live in front of an audience. For more Moth stories, check out the Moth's great, free, weekly podcast at themoth.org. And The Moth is starting its own radio show, The Moth Radio Hour, which will be coming to many public radio stations this August.
<v Ira Glass>杰夫·西默蒙，他在他的博客 andiamnotlying.com 上写其他故事。一如既往地感谢《飞蛾》，它使用在观众面前现场讲述的个人故事。想听更多《飞蛾》故事，查看《飞蛾》免费、精彩的每周播客 themoth.org。《飞蛾》正在开启自己的广播节目，《飞蛾电台一小时》，今年八月将在许多公共广播电台播出。

00:49:54.740 --> 00:50:10.540
<v Ira Glass>Act 4, Underling Gets An Underling. This is the story of somebody in a job that sounds like the kind of thing that would be, sort of, exciting. Stef Willen worked on a bunch of reality TV shows, but she was a production assistant, a PA, which is the lowest rung on the ladder. She did a lot of emptying trash cans.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“下属有了下属”。这是一个人的故事，他在一份听起来很令人兴奋的工作中工作。斯特夫·威伦在许多真人秀节目中工作，但她是一名制作助理（PA），这是梯队的最底层。她做了很多清空垃圾桶的工作。

00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:39.410
<v Stef Willen>I remember running around town with the weirdest lists of stuff to get, like toilet paper with a specific pattern on it. I've had one boss, she would do things like ask me to hang curtains in her office. And I was like, "Oh, but your wall is made of concrete." And she was like, "Oh, you can do it." You know? Stuff like that. I would be given these bizarre tasks, and if you didn't do them right, there was always this sense of are you stupid?
<v Stef Willen>我记得在城里跑来跑去拿最奇怪的东西清单，比如特定图案的卫生纸。我有一个老板，她会做一些事情，比如让我去她的办公室挂窗帘。我说，“哦，但你的墙是混凝土做的。”她说，“哦，你能做到的。”你知道？诸如此类的事情。我会接到这些奇怪的任务，如果你没做对，总有一种“你是不是蠢”的感觉。

00:50:39.410 --> 00:50:46.880
<v Ira Glass>And so the way understand it is that towards the end you came up with a plan. Can I ask you to just describe the plan that you came up with?
<v Ira Glass>所以我理解的是，临近最后你提出了一个计划。我可以请你描述一下你提出的那个计划吗？

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:58.740
<v Stef Willen>I just thought it would be hilarious if I came in the next day with an eager young person who was my production assistant.
<v Stef Willen>我只是觉得如果我第二天带一个渴望学习的年轻人，作为我的制作助理，那会很搞笑。

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:01.370
<v Ira Glass>Oh. So you would be a PA and you would have your own PA.
<v Ira Glass>哦。所以你成了制作助理，然后你有你自己的制作助理。

00:51:01.370 --> 00:51:12.510
<v Stef Willen>Exactly. Which is really unnecessary. It's a gopher with a gopher, so I don't know. I wanted to make a point. It'd just be like a little sweet revenge, you know?
<v Stef Willen>完全正确。这真的很没必要。这是一个跑腿的人带着另一个跑腿的人，所以我不知道。我想表达一个观点。那就像是一点甜蜜的复仇，你知道吗？

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:12.760
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:13.830
<v Stef Willen>I don't know.
<v Stef Willen>我不知道。

00:51:13.830 --> 00:51:16.400
<v Ira Glass>No. No. I understand. You'd be upsetting the natural order.
<v Ira Glass>不。不。我理解。你正在打破自然秩序。

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:32.080
<v Stef Willen>Right. Totally. It's like, if you can get someone under you, it's simple math, but you are not at the bottom. I just knew that I was somehow taking control over what was happening in my life if I could put someone just right under me.
<v Stef Willen>对。完全正确。这就像，如果你能把你下面的人搞定，这就是简单的数学，但你不再是底层了。我只是知道，如果我能把我下面的人搞定，我就以某种方式掌控了生活中发生的事情。

00:51:32.080 --> 00:51:37.180
<v Ira Glass>So, OK. So you're a PA hiring a PA. How'd you go about it?
<v Ira Glass>所以，好了。所以你是一名制作助理雇佣了一名制作助理。你是怎么做的？

00:51:37.180 --> 00:52:01.280
<v Stef Willen>I wrote up a Craigslist seeking a production assistant on a popular reality TV show. For the ability, I said must possess a medium work ethic, the ability to take out trash, and then sit for hours and work for free. And 21 people responded. 21 people. I was amazed.
<v Stef Willen>我在 Craigslist 上写了一个招聘，招聘一个热门真人秀节目的制作助理。关于能力，我说必须具备中等的工作道德，有能力倒垃圾，然后坐上几个小时免费工作。21个人回复了。21个人。我惊呆了。

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:30.490
<v Stef Willen>I ended up going with this guy, I'll call him Adam. It was interesting, I hired him because I actually felt like he might be slightly delinquent. When we had our, quote, unquote, "interview," he never turned down his car radio. And it was that kind of thing. And I'm like, OK. Well, we're going to meet at the coffee shop and we're going to drive to set. "OK." I'm like, "Do you want to get out a pen and a paper and write this down?" "Oh, OK."
<v Stef Willen>我最终选择了这个家伙，我叫他亚当。很有趣，我雇佣他是因为我确实觉得他可能稍微有点问题少年。当我们面试的时候，他从来没关过车里的收音机。就是那种事。我说，好吧。我们要在一个咖啡店见面，然后我们要开车去片场。“好的。”我说，“你要拿一支笔和纸记下来吗？”“哦，好的。”

00:52:30.490 --> 00:52:37.500
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow. So he's really a real beginner. Like, he really was not necessarily ready for the responsibility of a--
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。所以他真的是个新手。他并不一定准备好承担……的责任。

00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:39.710
<v Stef Willen>Of a phone conversation.
<v Stef Willen>电话通话的责任。

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:40.240
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Or a job.
<v Ira Glass>是的。或者工作。

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:51.440
<v Stef Willen>Yeah. I met him at a coffee shop. And he was this nice looking young guy. And he was wearing this argyle sweater and this scarf and a beret. And I was like, "Oh, my gosh. What did I just do?"
<v Stef Willen>是的。我在咖啡店见了他。他是一个好看的年轻人。他穿着这件菱形针织衫，围着围巾，戴着贝雷帽。我说，“天哪。我刚做了什么？”

00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:52.450
<v Ira Glass>A beret?
<v Ira Glass>贝雷帽？

00:52:52.450 --> 00:53:41.030
<v Stef Willen>A beret. Yeah. He followed me in his car to set, and I was getting a little nervous because I actually hadn't planned anything past this point. The first person we see is the line producer, and she was frantic, as always. You know, "Come on. Come on. We've got a big day. We've got to get going." And I said, "Oh, well you'll be glad to know I have some help. This is Adam, and he is my production assistant for today." And she just sort of stopped and looked at me. And she goes, "Well, good. We need the extra help." And there was no Stef is a genius. Or, oh look what Steff did. It was, literally, like oh, thank you. Oh, we need the help. How did you get him to work for free?
<v Stef Willen>贝雷帽。是的。他开车跟着我去片场，我开始有点紧张，因为我确实没计划这一步之后的事情。我们见到的第一个人是制片经理，她一如既往地疯狂。你知道，“快点。快点。我们有重要的一天。我们得出发了。”我说，“哦，好吧，你会很高兴知道我有一些帮手。这是亚当，他是今天我的制作助理。”她停下看我。她说，“好吧，很好。我们需要额外的帮助。”没有那种“斯特夫是个天才”或者“哦，看斯特夫做了什么”。它是，真的，就像“哦，谢谢。哦，我们需要帮助。”你是怎么让他免费工作的？

00:53:41.030 --> 00:53:43.730
<v Ira Glass>And so you did introduce him to your bosses?
<v Ira Glass>所以你把他介绍给了你的老板？

00:53:43.730 --> 00:54:07.340
<v Stef Willen>Yeah. I introduced him to everybody. I mean, I don't know why I thought that they would learn something from it, but I totally underestimated I guess, the joke, but also their need for workers. They were in production mode. They're not stopping to look at what I'm trying to say. They're like, OK. Well, we're 10 minutes behind. We can use Adam over here in hair and makeup.
<v Stef Willen>是的。我把他介绍给了所有人。我的意思是，我不知道为什么我觉得他们会从中学到什么，但我完全低估了这个笑话，也低估了他们对工人的需要。他们处于制作模式。他们不停下来看我想说什么。他们说，好的。好吧，我们落后了10分钟。我们可以用亚当在发型和化妆那边。

00:54:07.340 --> 00:54:27.890
<v Ira Glass>So you were hoping that they would get the lesson of, you see, this whole system you have is so arbitrary? And, we're not just cogs in a machine. Like, I could be a boss. And the lesson they took was not only are you all cogs in a machine, but you're such a cog, we can't even see that you're talking. Like you're not even an animal making noise here. Like, OK. Now, hand me that other animal over there.
<v Ira Glass>所以你希望他们学到，“你看，你们拥有的整个系统是如此武断”这门课？而且，我们不仅仅是机器里的齿轮。就像，我也能成为老板。而他们学到的教训不仅是你们都是机器里的齿轮，而且你们是如此齿轮，我们甚至看不出你在说话。就像你甚至不是一个会发出声音的动物。就像，好的。现在，把那边那只动物递给我。

00:54:27.890 --> 00:54:53.730
<v Stef Willen>Yeah. Exactly. It's like, oh, two cogs for one. Awesome. We'll take this one, you know? I went from his boss to his sidekick to, I don't know, his buddy? I'd be like, oh, I got this trash can. So it would be really gross and disgusting, and I'm like, oh, I don't want to mess up his scarf. I made sure he ate first. Like, he got his lunch before me.
<v Stef Willen>是的。完全正确。这就像，哦，两个齿轮当一个用。太棒了。我们会用这个，你知道吗？我从他的老板变成了他的跟班，然后变成了，我不知道，他的伙伴？我会说，哦，我有这个垃圾桶。所以它会变得非常恶心和令人作呕，我想，哦，我不想弄脏他的围巾。我确保他先吃饭。就像，他比我先吃午饭。

00:54:53.730 --> 00:55:31.140
<v Stef Willen>At one point, we were all sitting around the table. We'd been sitting staring at each other for about two hours, because they were filming, with nothing to do. And I looked over at Adam, and he had taken his beret off and it was on his knee. And he was, sort of, slouched down, and he was moving M&M's across his plate, one by one, with his index finger. And I think he said, "I have never not done anything for this long a time." And I was like, "Well, you know, it did say in my ad the ability to sit for hours." And he laughed.
<v Stef Willen>有一次，我们都坐在桌子旁。我们盯着对方看了大约两个小时，因为他们在拍摄，无事可做。我看向亚当，他摘下贝雷帽放在膝盖上。他有点颓废，他正用食指把M&M豆一个一个移到盘子里。我想他说，“我从来没有这么长时间什么都不做。”我说，“好吧，你知道，我的广告上确实写了具备坐几个小时的能力。”他笑了。

00:55:31.140 --> 00:56:11.880
<v Stef Willen>But shortly after, I told him to go. I was like, "Well, you know, you did a great job today. Definitely send me your resume." He kept in touch, like, he would email, do you know so-and-so? Or just little questions, you know? And then, at one point, he stopped asking me questions. And I got this text from him at 8:26 AM, and it said, "I want you." Period. "I want your body." Period. "Right now." Period. And I was like, OK.
<v Stef Willen>但不久之后，我让他走了。我说，“好吧，你知道，你今天做得很好。一定要把简历发给我。”他保持联系，就像，他会发邮件，你认识某某某吗？或者只是小问题，你知道吗？然后，有一次，他停止问我问题了。我在早上8:26收到他的短信，他说，“我要你。”句号。“我要你的身体。”句号。“现在。”句号。我说，好吧。

00:56:11.880 --> 00:56:14.650
<v Ira Glass>What did that say to you?
<v Ira Glass>那对你说了什么？

00:56:14.650 --> 00:56:19.870
<v Stef Willen>That he had not taken me seriously at all.
<v Stef Willen>他根本没把我当回事。

00:56:19.870 --> 00:56:24.220
<v Ira Glass>So you got him into your life because nobody else took you seriously, and then even he doesn't take you seriously.
<v Ira Glass>所以你把他带进你的生活是因为没人把你当回事，然后连他都不把你当回事。

00:56:24.220 --> 00:56:37.030
<v Stef Willen>Right. It just, sort of, made me laugh. I thought, OK. Well, he wants my body right now, which, 8:26 AM, that wasn't a good time for me.
<v Stef Willen>对。它只是，让我笑了。我想，好吧。好吧，他现在想要我的身体，这，早上8:26，对我来说不是个好时间。

00:56:37.030 --> 00:57:02.970
<v Ira Glass>Stef Willen, she has quit her PA jobs. She starred in the independent film, M, which won last year's Seattle International Film Festival, and she's writing a book.
<v Ira Glass>斯特夫·威伦，她已经辞去了制作助理的工作。她主演了独立电影《M》，该片赢得了去年的西雅图国际电影节奖项，她正在写一本书。

00:57:02.970 --> 00:57:41.470
<v Ira Glass>Well, the program was produced today by Sarah Koenig and myself with Alex Blumberg, Sean Cole, Jane Feltes, Lisa Pollak, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our Senior Producer is Julie Snyder. Our production help from Andy Dixon and Aaron Scott. Seth Lind is our Production Manager. Music help from Jessica Hopper. And this is our last show with our very precocious intern, Andi Dixon, who we all wish the best to, whether she ends up in grad school, or a job, or what's most likely, both.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，今天的节目由萨拉·科尼格和我制作，还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、肖恩·科尔、简·费尔特斯、丽莎·波拉克、阿莉莎·希普和南希·厄普代克。我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。我们的制作帮助来自安迪·迪克森和亚伦·斯科特。塞思·林德是我们的制作经理。音乐帮助来自杰西卡·霍珀。这是我们与非常早熟的实习生安迪·迪克森的最后一期节目，我们祝愿她一切顺利，无论她是去读研究生、找工作，还是最可能的，两者兼顾。

00:57:41.470 --> 00:57:46.470
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
